course: adult-life
lesson: 33
title: Neighbours, the council, rules and fairness
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Living close to other people means sharing streets, walls, parking spaces and rules. Most of the time it goes smoothly, but small things like a hedge or a parked car can grow into big rows.
explain: The council is the local government. It fixes roads, empties bins, runs parks and libraries, and decides on planning: whether people may build big extensions. Councillors are elected by local people.
explain: Scene: in Auckland, Dad stands over three bins holding a yoghurt pot: «Which one does this go in?» Then he finds a note on the car: «You've parked across my drive AGAIN!» He grumbles, then knocks next door and sorts it out in two minutes. A friendly word early stops a big row later.
explain: Rules and laws are shared agreements so everyone can live together safely. Fair does not always mean the same for everyone; sometimes it means each person gets what they need. Rights belong to everyone, and freedom stops where it starts to hurt others.
fact: Neighbours sometimes disagree about hedges, fences, noise or rubbish, and most disputes are solved by a friendly chat.
fact: The council is the local government that looks after roads, parks, bins, libraries and planning, run by elected councillors.
fact: People must ask the council for planning permission before building something big, and neighbours can object.
fact: Parking rows are common, and knocking politely usually works better than an angry note.
fact: Councils have recycling rules about which bin takes plastic, paper, glass or food, and the rules differ from place to place.
fact: If the wrong things go in a recycling bin, a whole load can be spoiled.
fact: Most people accept tax as the price of public services; they argue about how much and what it is spent on.
fact: Rules and laws are shared agreements; people follow them even when they dislike them, and change bad ones through proper ways.
fact: Sometimes fair means everyone gets the same, and sometimes it means people get what they need.
fact: Rights are things every person should have just for being human; children have special rights under a United Nations agreement.
fact: A free country gives people many freedoms, like speaking and voting, but freedom still has limits set by law.
fact: Your freedom stops where it starts to hurt other people.
exercise: Dad: «That hedge is a foot over our side, I'm writing to them.» What is going on? | A disagreement between neighbours | A competition for the best garden | A letter to thank the neighbours | A plan to sell the house
exercise: Mum: «Ring the council about that pothole.» Why the council? | It looks after local roads | It owns every car in town | It runs the national army | It sells holes to builders
exercise: A notice on a lamppost: «Planning application: two-storey extension.» What does it mean? | Someone asked the council to build a big extension | Someone lost a two-storey dog | The lamppost will be made taller | The street will be closed for two years while it is dug up
exercise: A note on a windscreen: «You've parked across my drive AGAIN!» What usually works better than a note? | Knocking and asking politely | Writing an even angrier note | Letting the tyres down at night | Parking across their drive back
exercise: Dad can't tell which bin the yoghurt pot goes in. Where can he check? | The council's recycling list | The supermarket's price list | The weather forecast | The bus timetable
exercise: Why does putting the wrong thing in the recycling bin matter? | A whole load can be spoiled | The bin lorry will refuse to stop forever | It is against the law to own a yoghurt | The bins will catch fire straight away
exercise: «I don't mind paying tax as long as it's spent properly.» What does she mean? | She accepts tax but cares how it is used | She never wants to pay any tax | She wants all tax spent on herself | She thinks tax should be doubled at once
exercise: Teacher: «I know you don't like it, but rules are rules.» What is the idea? | Rules are followed even when we dislike them | Rules only count if you like them | Teachers can make any rule at all that they happen to want | Rules are just suggestions to ignore
exercise: Your sister gets more pudding: «It's not fair!» What changes things best? | Calmly explaining why it isn't fair | Shouting louder until you win | Taking her pudding when she looks away | Refusing to eat anything ever again
exercise: A school poster: «Every child has the right to be heard.» What is a right? | Something every person should have for being human | A prize given only to the best-behaved pupils in the class | A rule that only grown-ups can use | Something you buy at the school shop
exercise: Older boy: «I can do what I want, it's a free country!» Is he right? | No, freedom stops where it hurts other people | Yes, in a free country there are no laws | Yes, but only on buses and trains | No, there are no free countries at all
exercise: Who runs the council? | Councillors elected by local people | The Prime Minister alone | The police and the fire brigade together | Whoever owns the biggest house in the area
exercise: Is fair always the same for everyone? | No, sometimes fair means what each person needs | Yes, fair always means exactly the same for everybody | No, fair means the oldest always wins | Yes, fair means whatever Mum says first
exercise: A rule seems silly to you. What can you do? | Ask why it exists and politely suggest a change | Break it every day until it is gone | Ignore it because silly rules don't count | Shout at the person who made it
exercise: Can a child write to the council? | Yes, for example about a broken swing | No, only grown-ups who pay tax may write | Yes, but only to say thank you | No, the council never reads letters
exercise: Why are there planning rules? | To stop buildings that block light or are unsafe | To make every house in the street look exactly the same | To stop anyone ever building anything | To make the council rich from fines
test: Your brother says «The council is just the people who empty the bins.» Put him right. | It's the local government for roads, parks, bins and more | Yes, the council only empties the bins and does nothing else | No, the council runs the whole country | No, the council is a group of teachers
test: Overheard over the fence: «Your music, 2am, again. I'm calling the council.» What is this? | A neighbour dispute about noise | A party invitation for next week | A thank-you for the lovely music | A plan to start a band together
test: Dad's mate: «Taxes are too high, mate.» Mum's friend: «Too low, the hospitals need more.» Which is true? | Both are reasonable views, honestly held | One of them must be a bad person | Taxes have nothing to do with hospitals | Only the lower view is allowed by law
test: Your little sister has a broken arm and gets help cutting her food, but you don't. Is that fair? | Yes, fair can mean getting what you need | No, fair means everybody gets the same | No, she should cut yours too | Yes, because younger children always win
test: Kid in the playground: «Free country, I can shove who I like.» What's wrong with that? | Freedom stops where it hurts other people | Nothing, shoving is free in a free country | Only teachers can be shoved freely | Free countries have no rules at school
test: If nobody needed planning permission, what might happen? | Someone could build a tower that blocks your light | Every house would get bigger for free | Nothing would change at all | The council would build everyone a garden at the back of the house
test: Mum, reading the bin calendar: «Glass is Tuesdays now, they've changed it again, for crying out loud.» What does she have to do? | Follow the council's new recycling day | Throw the glass in the river instead | Keep the glass until it changes back | Ring the PM to complain about glass
test: Gossip: «They put in for an extension and half the street objected.» What did the neighbours do? | Told the council they disagreed with the plan | Helped to build the neighbour's extension for free | Moved away from the street at once | Painted the house to stop it
test: A friend says «Rights are just for grown-ups.» Is that right? | No, children have rights too, under a UN agreement | Yes, children get rights when they turn 18 | Yes, only adults who vote have rights | No, only children have rights, not adults
test: The new kid says rules at school are pointless. What is a good answer? | They help everyone live and learn together safely | They are there to make teachers feel powerful and important | They are pointless, so you can ignore them | They only matter when the head is watching
test: Dad, after knocking next door: «Sorted. He's moving his car, nice bloke actually.» What does this show? | A polite word solved the problem quickly | Angry notes are the best way to win | Neighbours always become enemies | Dad had to call the police in the end
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the functions of a local council? | It maintains roads, parks, libraries and refuse collection and determines planning | It commands the armed forces and conducts foreign relations | It is a private company which sells houses to residents | It is a court which sentences persons for criminal offences
exam: Identify the principal reason for the requirement of planning permission. | To prevent construction which is unsafe or detrimental to neighbours | To ensure that every dwelling in a street is of an identical appearance | To prohibit the construction of any building whatsoever | To guarantee that every resident pays an additional fee
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning freedom in a free country. | It is extensive but limited by law where it harms others | It is unlimited and permits any conduct whatsoever | It is granted solely to persons who have attained eighteen | It is confined to the act of voting at elections
exam: It is given that a younger sibling with an injured arm receives assistance at meals. Determine which conception of fairness is applied. | Fairness according to need | Fairness according to age alone | Identical treatment of every person | Fairness determined by the loudest complaint
